<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merogaisus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 306</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Merogaisus was a Frankish king who, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascaric">Ascaric</a>, broke the peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> around 306. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> captured both kings and had them thrown to the beasts in the amphitheatre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, an event praised in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> panegyric of 310. Constantine followed it with punitive campaigns across the Rhine against the Bructeri.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merogaisus.jpg" alt="Merogaisus" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Kühn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphietheather_von_Trier_gross.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
